          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. Her qualification measures that love rather than retracting the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The expression, as qualified in the same sentence, invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond and affirms that her love is governed by it. Her ensuing explanation identifies the bond's practical duties—returning Lear's parental care through obedience, love, and honour. This renews or affirms an existing relational obligation rather than making a new promise with future terms.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"You have begot me, bred me, lov’d me: I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression is a measured answer to Lear's demand, explicitly limited by Cordelia's bond. It is not presented as a reflex-like affective outburst or expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and affirmation of filial obligation, the expression functions as a principled, corrective refusal to exaggerate love for advantage in Lear's public contest. Cordelia answers his demand while limiting her claim to exactly what her bond warrants.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks each daughter to declare how much she loves him and links the answer to her inheritance. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives the focal avowal as a restrained alternative to her sisters' expansive declarations, grounding it in her filial bond rather than claiming exclusive or limitless love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms an existing filial undertaking: to return the duties appropriate to Lear as the father who begot, raised, and loved her, specifically through obedience, love, and honour. The focal expression does not create a new promise; “According to my bond” positions her as already bound. Lear does not accept this formulation as adequate uptake: he tells her to “Mend your speech a little” and warns that her fortunes are at risk. Later supplied scenes support enduring affection and care, but those later actions are not themselves the source of the original undertaking force.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as William Shakespeare's King Lear, including the opening love test and Cordelia's later reconciliation with Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The language and later supplied conduct strongly support genuine affection, but literary evidence cannot conclusively establish an inward state. The precise full scope of Cordelia's filial “bond” is not specified, and Lear's rejection prevents mutual acceptance of her formulation in this scene.\"}"
        }
